---
import { states, stateOptions } from "../../lib/states";

const stages = [
  ["Scrape", "Fetch", "Ask for each page, and write down which answered", "1, this one"],
  ["Store", "Archive", "Save each page untouched, before any code reads it", "2"],
  ["", "Prove", "Make each page show it is the school and season asked for", "3"],
  ["Parse", "Parse", "Turn the page's tables into typed records", "4"],
  ["Build, in part", "Resolve", "One identity per team, one record per match", "5 and 6"],
  ["", "Check", "Gates that can stop a bad record", "7"],
  ["Build, in part", "Build", "Publish the site, the database and the journals", "8 and 9"],
  ["Report", "The coverage record", "Kept from Fetch onward, beside every step", "1, this one"],
];

const learnPrompt = `I'm learning how to tell whether a data collection is complete. Teach me with one running example that is not about sports.

1. What an expected set is, and why it has to be written before the collection runs and from outside it.
2. Coverage as what arrived divided by what was expected, and why the unit I count in (sources, records, rows) changes the answer.
3. The difference between a source that is empty, one I could not reach, and one I have no way to read, and what each lets me claim.
4. Missing completely at random, missing at random, and missing not at random, with one example of each.
5. End with three questions that check my understanding. Wait for my answers before you give yours.`;

const q = [
  { key: "timeout", text: "Pinecrest's results page timed out on every try.", answer: "unavailable", why: "Unavailable. It was requested and didn't arrive, and the reason is recorded." },
  { key: "header", text: "Oakfield's forfeits page arrived, confirms this season, and its table has a header with no rows.", answer: "empty", why: "Empty. Fetched and verified, and it genuinely contains no rows, so zero forfeits is true." },
  { key: "scans", text: "The league posts its judges' score sheets as scanned images, and the collector has no way to read them.", answer: "no-collector", why: "No collector. Retrying won't help until someone builds a reader for that format." },
];
const captions = [
  ["found", "22 debates"],
  ["scheduled", "22 of 30 debates"],
  ["named", "22 of 27 finished debates. Pinecrest's page did not answer."],
  ["whole", "27 of 27 finished debates. Pinecrest's five were never held."],
];
---

<section id="coverage">
  <h2>The idea: coverage</h2>
  <p>All three voicemails point back to one concept. Here it is up front so you can spot it in each case.</p>

  <div class="cv-define">
    <p><dfn>Completeness</dfn> asks: is everything here? <dfn>Coverage</dfn> is how you measure it, by comparing what actually arrived against what you expected to find.</p>
    <p class="cv-formula"><span>coverage</span> = <span>what arrived</span> ÷ <span>what was expected</span>, counted in the same unit</p>
  </div>
  <p>Three rules keep that ratio honest:</p>
  <ul class="cv-three">
    <li><b>Define the expected set first, from outside the run.</b><span>Write the list of what should exist before fetching, using an independent source like a membership list or competition format. If you build the denominator from what arrived, silent sources disappear from their own count.</span></li>
    <li><b>Count in the unit of your claim.</b><span>Count pages and one silent site is 1 gap. Count matches and that same site may cost 7. Always match the unit to the claim you are making.</span></li>
    <li><b>Assign a state to every expected item.</b><span>A collector produces two outputs: the data itself, and a <strong>coverage record</strong> listing every expected item and what happened to it.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <p>Every item you request ends in one of four states:</p>
  <div class="cv-states">
    {states.map((s) => (
      <div class={`cv-state is-${s.key}`}>
        <h4><i aria-hidden="true"></i>{s.name}</h4>
        <p>{s.means}</p>
        <p><b>What you can claim:</b> {s.claim}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p>Both <em>unavailable</em> and <em>no collector</em> mean the value is <strong>unknown</strong>: the first might clear on a retry, while the second requires new code. Anything left off your list has no state at all. It was <strong>never asked for</strong>, so no log will ever mention it. Unknown is never zero.</p>

  <details class="cv-more">
    <summary>How Kick's 5 stages map to the Touchline pipeline</summary>
    <p>The video showed Kick's five stages. The full Touchline platform uses seven steps across the course, and Kick's “Build” stage combines two of them.</p>
    <div class="cv-scroll">
      <table class="cv-stagemap">
        <caption>Kick's stages compared to the platform's steps</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kick</th><th scope="col">Platform</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Module</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {stages.map(([kick, step, what, mod]) => (
            <tr class:list={{ "is-skipped": !kick, "is-here": mod.startsWith("1,") }}><td>{kick || <span class="cv-none">skipped</span>}</td><th scope="row">{step}</th><td>{what}</td><td>{mod}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Kick's <em>Build</em> chose which copy of each match to keep and generated the table. Touchline's <em>Build</em> publishes finished products only from checked records.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="cv-learn-box">
    <summary>Want another example? Open an AI study prompt</summary>
    <div class="cv-prompt cv-learn">
      <p class="cv-prompt-note">Paste this into any AI assistant for a short walkthrough outside sports, then come back to test yourself below.</p>
      <p data-copy-text tabindex="0">{learnPrompt}</p>
      <button type="button" data-copy>Copy prompt</button>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h4 class="cv-try">Check your understanding: a debate league</h4>
  <p>A school debate league has six schools, and each hosts every other school once. Three debates haven't happened yet. A collector fetches each school's results page and reads each debate from its host's page. Five pages arrive with 22 finished debates. Pinecrest's site times out, and Pinecrest has hosted five finished debates.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="idea">
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer="30" data-why="30. Six hosts, each with five visitors: 6 × 5, based on the league's format.">
      <label for="cv-idea-total">How many debates are scheduled for the full season?</label>
      <input id="cv-idea-total" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="idea-total" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    {q.map((item) => (
      <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer={item.answer} data-why={item.why}>
        <label><span>{item.text} What state is it in?</span>
          <select data-save={`idea-${item.key}`}><option value="">Choose a state</option>{stateOptions.map(([k, n]) => <option value={k}>{n}</option>)}</select>
        </label>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="named" data-why="22 of 27 finished, with the silent page named. Not 30, because three debates haven't happened yet. Not 27 of 27: Pinecrest's debates happened, and we haven't read them." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ found: "A raw count with no denominator hides what's missing.", scheduled: "Three of the 30 haven't happened yet, so they can't be missing.", whole: "Pinecrest's five debates happened. Its page is unavailable: unknown, not zero." })}>
      <label><span>Which caption belongs under the league table?</span>
        <select data-save="idea-caption"><option value="">Choose</option>{captions.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer all five, then check your answers.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>That's the whole framework: an expected set defined outside the run, a count in the unit of your claim, and a state for every expected item. Next, let's see what broke when Kick skipped each one.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="cv-next">Next: Case 1, the expected set Kick never wrote.</p>
</section>
